Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Bulwark**, a heavy bipedal war-mech that braces a broad
tower shield on its left arm, swings a heavy siege maul in its right, and strides
forward on two legs — as a 40x60x30 opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim`
tool. You build it by **painting discrete opaque cells** — one recorded operation at a
time — and you **author its walk and weapon animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the Bulwark is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how
you break the mech into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape
every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a walking, smashing
mech needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json` —
together with the rig you define are your submission.

Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is sculpted
**separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**. Voxels are
**opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. Each call only records the operation to that part's log and renders
**nothing** — run `voxel-anim render` to (re)draw the previews and the assembled
scene (`--component <part>` redraws one part; `--time <ms> --animation <name>`
renders the model posed at an instant of an animation), and you **must** run
`voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry the
run is scored from — an unrendered part is scored as empty.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `voxel-anim define-part`
(giving each a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and
author the required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel
operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt
into it; each part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates. The volume starts
**empty**.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name body
voxel-anim fill-box --part body --x0 10 --y0 30 --z0 8 --x1 29 --y1 52 --z1 22 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining
parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`parts/<part>.png` after you run `voxel-anim render`** to see that part's progress, **and read the
assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole mech composed from all your parts,
drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the legs seat beneath the body and reach the ground and the
tower shield meets the left shoulder. Because each part is sculpted in the shared
volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the finished mech; the
scene simply composes them there.

